# Implementation Workflow Governance

- Follow sequence: `plan -> interfaces -> implementation -> tests -> evidence -> docs update`.
- Before coding, confirm target module boundary, acceptance criteria, and rollback/fallback in `.local/control-center/plan.md`.
- Treat `.local/control-center/*` markdown files as live implementation state.
- Before coding, read:
  - `.local/control-center/plan.md`
  - `.local/control-center/work-tracker.md`
  - `.local/control-center/architecture.md`
  - `.local/control-center/test-plan.md`
  - `.local/control-center/test-index.md`
- For each slice, set exactly one primary task to `in_progress` in `.local/control-center/work-tracker.md`.
- Do not start implementation unless scope and acceptance criteria are present in `.local/control-center/plan.md`.
- Include tests for happy path, failure path, and retry/timeout behavior when relevant.
- Require deterministic replay tests for high-risk workflows and tool side effects.
- Keep changes incremental and reversible; avoid big-bang rewrites unless explicitly approved.
- Keep code simple (KISS).
- After each substantive code change, update:
  - task status in `.local/control-center/work-tracker.md`
  - test ownership/coverage state in `.local/control-center/test-index.md` when tests/modules changed
  - testing priorities/gaps in `.local/control-center/test-plan.md` when risk or scope changed
  - `.local/control-center/updates-log.md` with change summary, impact, blockers, and next step
- When blocked, explicitly mark blockers and required unblock action in `.local/control-center/work-tracker.md`.
- Keep control-center updates synchronized with code state and validation outcomes.
- Block release when any P0 security/quality/reliability gate fails.
- Require CI/CD evidence bundle links for release candidates.
